<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">François Mackandal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1730–<a href="/years/1758">1758</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Makandal, Macandal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">François Mackandal was a maroon and spiritual leader in the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> (present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>), sometimes described as a vodou priest (houngan). Born in Central or Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and enslaved young, he is thought to have lost his right arm in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> press, and from around 1748 he lived as a maroon, making protective charms and holding rituals among the enslaved. Amid a panic among enslavers over poisoning, he was captured in 1758, accused of joining the maroons to kill slave owners, and burned at the stake without ever confessing; recent scholarship disputes that he ever used poison to kill. His case was seen as a precursor to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Mackandal</b>, a one-armed Vodou houngan who escaped in 1751, united many bands, raided for six years, reputedly killed more than 6,000 people and preached the killing of the whites; he was burned alive at Cap-Français in 1758.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Haitian folklore holds that it was sparked by a Vodou ritual at Bois-Caïman in 1791 and that the early revolutionaries Boukman and <b>François Mackandal</b> were oungans, though historians debate these claims.</blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1758 the authorities burned alive <b>Mackandal</b>, a one-armed maroon whom they accused of plotting to poison the planters.</blockquote><a href="/societies/saint-domingue">Social Hierarchy of Saint-Domingue</a></li></ul></section>
